The first triage result is important: the paper does not claim computational hardness for finding a coloring. Its central generalized-prism theorem is constructive, so a Track A claim would be inappropriate unless the generated distribution had independent hardness evidence. I’m now checking whether the paper offers a legitimate Track B compression gap—especially its explicit subdivision colorings—rather than mistaking “an algorithm exists” for a rejection.
