   605	\section{A class larger than generalized prisms}
   606	\label{sec:more-general}
   607	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   608	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   609	
   610	In this section we confirm Conjecture~\ref{con:main} for a class of graphs larger than generalized prisms of cycles by proving that if $G$ is a connected, cubic graph of order $2n$ with a $2$-factor $\mathcal{F}$ and a perfect matching $M$, where $\mathcal{F}$ contains a cycle $C$ of length $n$, no edge of $M$ has both vertices in $C$, and $\mathcal{F}$ contains at most one $5$-cycle, then $G$ is $(1,1,2,2)$-colorable. In other words, our result extends Theorem~\ref{thm:onecycle} to the graphs obtained from generalized prisms in such a way that one of the two $n$-cycles in the edge set of a generalized prism is replaced by a union of cycles among which at most one is a 5-cycle.
   611	
   612	\begin{theorem} \label{thm:2connectedcubic}
   613	Let $G$ be a connected, cubic graph of order $2n$ with a $2$-factor $\mathcal{F}$ and a perfect matching $M$.
   614	If $\mathcal{F}$ contains a cycle $C$ of length $n$ where no edge of $M$ has both vertices in $C$, and $\mathcal{F}$ contains at
   615	most one $5$-cycle, then $G$ is $(1,1,2,2)$-colorable.
   616	\end{theorem}
   617	
   618	\proof
   619	Note that by Theorem~\ref{thm:onecycle}, we may assume that $\mathcal{F}$ contains at least three cycles.
   620	Thus, $n \ge 6$. We let $C_n=x_1\cdots x_n$ represent the cycle in $\mathcal{F}$ of order $n$, and let $Z_1, \dots, Z_k$ be
   621	the remaining cycles of $\mathcal{F}$.  Reindexing if necessary, we may assume that if $\mathcal{F}$ contains a $5$-cycle that $Z_1$ is said $5$-cycle. Otherwise, if $Z_i$ is odd for some $i \in [k]$, we let $Z_1$ represent the smallest odd cycle among all $Z_i$, $i \in [k]$. In any case, we let $Z_1 = y_1\cdots y_p$ for some $3 \le p <n$.
   622	
   623	Assume first that $Z_1$ is a $5$-cycle so that $p = 5$. Note that there exists $x_i \in C_n$ such that
   624	$f(x_i) \in Z_1$ and $f(x_j) \not\in Z_1$ for some $j \in \{i-1, i+1\}$. Reindexing $x_1, \dots, x_n$ if necessary, we may
   625	assume $f(x_n) \in Z_1$ and, redrawing $G$ if necessary, $f(x_{n-1}) \not\in Z_1$.
   626	
   627	As in Theorem~\ref{thm:onecycle}, we let $X_1 = \{x_n\}$, $X_2 = \{x_i \mid 1 \le i < n, i \text{ is odd}\}$ and $X_3 = \{x_i \mid 2 \le i <n, i \text{ is even}\}$. In what follows, we partition the vertices of $\cup_{i=1}^k Z_i$ into $Y_1, Y_2, Y_3$, and let $V_i = X_i \cup Y_i$ for each $i \in [3]$. In each case, $G^2[V_1]$ will be bipartite and $V_2, V_3$ will be independent sets.
   628	
   629	\noindent{\bf Case 1.} Suppose that $f(x_1) \in Z_1$. Without loss of generality, we may assume $f(x_1) = y_1$, and
   630	reindexing $Z_1$ if necessary, $f(x_n) = y_s$ where $s \in \{4,5\}$. For each $i \in \{2, \dots, k\}$ let $T_i$ be one of the sets $A_1$ or $A_2$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:help} depending on the congruence class of $n$ modulo $4$. Note that for each $i \in \{2, \dots, k\}$, $G^2[T_i]$ is bipartite.
   631	
   632	Next, we assume for the time being that $s = 4$ and let $T_1 = \{ y_2, y_5\}$. We let
   633	\begin{itemize}
   634	\item $Y_1 = \cup_{i=1}^k T_i$,
   635	\item $Y_2 = W_2 \cup \bigcup_{i=2}^k \{v_j \in Z_i - T_i \mid f^{-1}(v_j) \in X_3\}$ and
   636	\item $Y_3 = W_3 \cup \bigcup_{i=2}^k \{v_j \in Z_i - T_i \mid f^{-1}(v_j) \in X_2\}$,
   637	\end{itemize}
   638	where
   639	\begin{itemize}
   640	\item $W_2 = \{y_3\}$ and $W_3 = \{y_1,y_4\}$, if $f^{-1}(y_3) \in X_3$;
   641	\item $W_2 = \{y_4\}$ and $W_3 = \{y_1, y_3\}$, if $f^{-1}(y_3) \in X_2$.
   642	\end{itemize}
   643	
   644	In $G^2[V_1]$, all edges incident to $x_n$ are bridges to either the $K_2$ induced by $T_1 - \{x_n\}$ or to a
   645	bipartite component induced by $T_i$ for some $i \in \{2, \dots, k\}$. Thus, $G^2[V_1]$ is bipartite.
   646	Furthermore, $V_i$ where $i \in \{2, 3\}$ is independent.
   647	
   648	Now, one can easily see that $Y_1, Y_2, Y_3$ can be defined in a similar fashion if instead $s = 5$. \smallqed
   649	
   650	\noindent{\bf Case 2.} Suppose that $f(x_1) \not\in Z_1$. Without loss of generality, we may assume $f(x_n) = y_1$. In this case,
   651	we define $T_i$ as in Case 1 for each $i \in \{2, \dots, k\}$ and we let $T_1 = \{y_2, y_4\}$. We let
   652	\begin{itemize}
   653	\item $Y_1 = \bigcup_{i=1}^k T_i$,
   654	\item $Y_2 = W_2 \cup \bigcup_{i=2}^k \{v_j \in Z_i - T_i \mid f^{-1}(v_j) \in X_3\}$ and
   655	\item $Y_3 = W_3 \cup \bigcup_{i=2}^k \{v_j \in Z_i - T_i \mid f^{-1}(v_j) \in X_2\}$,
   656	\end{itemize}
   657	where $y_3\in W_2$ if and only if $f^{-1}(y_3) \in X_3$, and otherwise $y_3\in W_3$; and $y_1$ and $y_5$ are in different sets $W_2,W_3$, depending on $f^{-1}(y_5)$.
   658	
   659	As in Case 1, $G^2[V_1]$ is bipartite and $V_i$ is independent for $i \in \{2,3\}$. \smallqed
   660	\smallskip
   661	
   662	Now consider the case, when at least one of the cycles $Z_i$ is odd and none of them is a $5$-cycle. Recall that $Z_1$ is a shortest odd cycle from $\mathcal{F}$. We shall assume that $f(x_n) = y_s$ for some $s \in [p]$. Whether or not $f(x_1) \in Z_1$, we may choose $T_1$ to be the set $A_3, A_4$, or $A_5$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:help} so that $\{y_s, y_{s+1}\}\cap T_1 = \emptyset$ if $f(x_1) \not\in Z_1$, $\{f(x_1), y_s, y_{s+1}\} \cap T_1 = \emptyset$ if $f(x_1) \in Z_1$, and $G^2[T_1 \cup X_1]$ is bipartite. Then for each $i \in \{2, \dots, k\}$, we let $T_i$ be one of the sets $A_1$ or $A_2$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:help} depending on the congruence class of $n$ modulo $4$. Defining $Y_1$, $Y_2$, and $Y_3$ similarly as in Case 1, one can verify that $G^2[V_1]$ is indeed bipartite.
   663	
   664	Finally, consider the case that $Z_1$ is even, in which case all the cycles $Z_i$ are even, and so $n$ is also even. In this and only in this case, we let $X_1=\emptyset$, and $X_2 = \{x_i \mid 1 \le i \le n-1, i \text{ is odd}\}$ and $X_3 = \{x_i \mid 2 \le i \le n, i \text{ is even}\}$. Next, for each $i \in [k]$, we let $T_i$ be the set $A_1$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:help} and we define $Y_1 =  \bigcup_{i=1}^k T_i$ (note that $V_1=Y_1$).
   665	Letting
   666	\begin{itemize}
   667	\item $Y_2 = \bigcup_{i=1}^k \{v_j \in Z_i - T_i \mid f^{-1}(v_j) \in X_3\}$ and
   668	\item $Y_3 = \bigcup_{i=1}^k \{v_j \in Z_i - T_i \mid f^{-1}(v_j) \in X_2\}$
   669	\end{itemize}
   670	we obtain a $(1,1,2,2)$-coloring of $G$.
   671	\qed
   672	
   673	Note that the graphs from Theorem~\ref{thm:2connectedcubic} are $2$-connected. We suspect that a similar approach might work to prove that an arbitrary 2-connected cubic graph (except the Petersen graph) has a $(1,1,2,2)$-packing coloring.
   674	(Recall that by Petersen's theorem~\cite{petersen} the edge set of any such graph can be partitioned into a 2-factor and a perfect matching.)
   675	
   676	One class of cubic graphs covered by the result in Theorem~\ref{thm:2connectedcubic} are some subclasses of generalized Petersen graphs.
   677	Let $k$ and $n$ be positive integers such that $k < n/2$.  The \emph{generalized Petersen graph} $P(n,k)$ has vertex set
   678	$\{u_1,v_1, \ldots, u_n,v_n\}$.  The edge set of $P(n,k)$ is the set
   679	\[ \{u_iu_{i+1} \mid i \in [n]\} \cup \{u_iv_i \mid i \in [n]\} \cup \{v_iv_{i+k} \mid i \in [n]\}\,,\]
   680	where addition on the subscripts is computed modulo $n$.  The set $\{u_i \mid i\in [n]\}$ induces a cycle of order $n$,
   681	while the set $\{v_i \mid i \in [n]\}$ induces a disjoint union of cycles.  The order and the number of this latter collection
   682	of cycles depends on the relationship between $n$ and $k$.  It is easy to see that if $n$ and $k$ are relatively prime,
   683	then $\{v_i \mid i \in [n]\}$ induces a single cycle of order $n$.  In this case $P(n,k)$ is a generalized prism of $C_n$ and satisfies the hypotheses of
   684	Theorem~\ref{thm:onecycle} unless $n=5$, in which case $P(5,k)$ is either the ordinary prism of $C_5$ (that is, the
   685	Cartesian product of $C_5$ and $K_2$) or the famous Petersen graph.  If $n$ and $k$ are not relatively prime, then the subgraph
   686	of $P(n,k)$ induced by $\{v_i \mid i \in [n]\}$ consists of the disjoint union of $n/r$ cycles each of order $r$, where
   687	$r$ is the smallest positive integer such that $rk$ is divisible by $n$.  Hence, these will be $5$-cycles if and only if
   688	$n$ is a multiple of $5$.
   689	\begin{corollary} \label{cor:GPG}
   690	If $n$ and $k$ are positive integers such that $k<n/2$ and $n$ is not a multiple of $5$, then $P(n,k)$ has a $(1,1,2,2)$-coloring and
   691	hence $\pch(S(P(n,k)))\le 5$.
   692	\end{corollary}
   693	
   694	
   695	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   696	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   697	\section{Multiple subdivisions}
   698	\label{sec:multiple}
   699	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   700	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   701	
   702	We have already remarked that $\pch(S(K_n))=n+1$. We next consider $\pch(S_i(K_n))$ for $i\ge 2$.
   703	
   704	\begin{proposition} \label{prop:SubofKn}
   705	If $n\ge 3$ and $i\ge 3$, then
   706	$$\pch(S_i(K_n))=\left\{
   707	  \begin{array}{ll}
   708	    3; & \hbox{if $i\equiv 3 \pmod 4  $,} \\
   709	    4; & \hbox{otherwise.}
   710	  \end{array}
   711	\right.$$
   712	Moreover, $\pch(S_2(K_n))\xrightarrow[n\to \infty]{}\infty$.
   713	\end{proposition}
   714	
   715	\proof
   716	Clearly, $\pch(S_i(K_n))\ge 3$ for $n\ge 3$ and $i\ge 3$. Note that $S_i(K_n)$ contains a cycle of length $3i + 3 = 3(i+1)$.
   717	Since $\pch(C_n) = 3$ if $n\equiv 0 \pmod 4$, and $\pch(C_n) = 4$ otherwise (see~\cite{goddard-2008}), we get that $\pch(S_i(K_n))\ge 3$ if $i\equiv 3\pmod 4$, and $\pch(S_i(K_n))\ge 4$ otherwise.
   718	
   719	To prove that these lower bounds are tight, we color $S_i(K_n)$ as follows. If $i\equiv 3\pmod 4$, then color the vertices $v\in V(K_n)$ with color $3$;  otherwise color all these vertices with $4$. Colorings of the subdivided vertices are done based on the parity of $i\pmod 4$ as follows. If $i\equiv 3 \pmod 4$, then for each original edge of $K_n$ color the subdivided vertices consecutively by $1,2,1$, and add the block of colors $3,1,2,1$ as many times as required. If $i=4$, use colors $1,2,3,1$. For any even $i\ge 6$, alternatively attach to the four colors $1,2,3,1$ the pairs $2,1$ and $3,1$ as many times as required. Finally, let $i\equiv 1 \pmod 4$. If $i = 5$, then use the pattern $1,3,1,2,1$, and if $i\ge 9$, then add the block $3,1,2,1$ as many times as required. In all of the cases it is straightforward to verify that the constructed colorings are packing colorings.
   720	
   721	It remains to consider the case $i=2$. If $e=uv\in E(K_n)$, then let $u_e$ and $v_e$ be the vertices of $S_2(K_n)$ obtained by subdividing the edge $e$, where $u_e$ is adjacent to $u$ and $v_e$ to $v$. Let $c$ be an arbitrary packing coloring of $S_2(K_n)$.  Then for any edge $e=uv\in E(K_n)$ we must have $c(u_e)\ne 1$ or $c(v_e)\ne 1$. Define now the orientation of $K_n$ as follows. If for the edge $e=uv$ we have $c(u_e)\ne 1$, then in $K_n$ orient the edge $uv$ from $u$ to $v$. Otherwise we must have $c(v_e)\ne 1$
   722	 in which case we orient the edge $uv$ from $v$ to $u$. (In the case that both $c(u_e)\ne 1$ and $c(v_e)\ne 1$ hold, we orient the edge $uv$ arbitrarily.) By the degree sum formula for digraphs and the pigeon-hole principle there exists a vertex $u$ with out-degree at least $\lceil (n-1)/2 \rceil$. This means that in $S_2(K_n)$ $u$ has at least that many neighbors colored with different colors bigger than $1$. Hence $\pch(S_2(K_n)) > \lceil (n-1)/2 \rceil$.
   723	\qed
   724	
   725	By using our earlier observation that the packing chromatic number of a subgraph is bounded above by the packing
   726	chromatic number of the original graph, we get the following immediate corollary of Proposition~\ref{prop:SubofKn}.
   727	
   728	
   729	\begin{corollary}
   730	\label{cor:subdivisiongraph}
   731	If $G$ is a connected graph of order at least $3$ and $i \ge 3$, then
   732	\[3 \le \pch(S_i(G)) \le 4\,.\]
   733	\end{corollary}
   734	
   735	In the case of trees we can further strengthen the result of  Corollary~\ref{cor:subdivisiongraph} by including the parameter $i=2$ (and $i=1$), and by showing that for any odd $i$, the packing chromatic number is always $3$. More precisely, we have the following result.
   736	
   737	\begin{theorem}
   738	\label{thm:subdivided-trees}
   739	If $i\ge 1$, then
   740	$$\max\{\pch(S_i(T))\ |\ T\ {\rm tree}\} = \left\{
   741	  \begin{array}{ll}
   742	    3; & i\ {\rm odd}, \\
   743	    4; & i\ {\rm even}.
   744	  \end{array}
   745	\right.$$
   746	\end{theorem}
   747	
   748	\proof
   749	Let $T$ be a tree on at least three vertices. Then, as already mentioned, $\pch(S_1(T))=3$, hence the assertion holds for $i=1$.
   750	
   751	Let $i=2$ and let $T$ be an arbitrary tree. To see that $\pch(S_2(T))\le 4$ let $v$ be an arbitrary vertex of $T$ and consider the BFS-tree of $S_2(T)$ rooted in $v$. Then set
   752	$$c(x) = \left\{
   753	  \begin{array}{ll}
   754	    1; & d_T(v,x) \equiv 1 \pmod 3, \\
   755	    2; & d_T(v,x) \equiv 2 \pmod 3, \\
   756	    3; & d_T(v,x) \equiv 0 \pmod 6, \\
   757	    4; & d_T(v,x) \equiv 3 \pmod 6. \\
   758	  \end{array}
   759	\right.$$
   760	It is straightforward to verify that $c$ is a packing coloring of $S_2(T)$. Let now $T$ be a tree with a vertex $u$ of degree at least $3$, let $v$ be a
   761	neighbor of $u$ and let $w$ be a neighbor of $v$ different from $u$.
   762	Recall that if $xy\in E(T)$, then we denote with $e_{xy}$ and $e_{yx}$ the vertices of $S_2(T)$ obtained by subdividing $xy$,
   763	where $e_{xy}$ is the vertex adjacent to $x$. Let $c$ be a packing coloring of $S_2(T)$. If $c(u) = 1$, then considering the neighbors of $u$ (in $S_2(T)$) we see that $\pch(S_2(T))\ge 4$. The same conclusion also follows if $\{c(u), c(v)\} = \{2,3\}$. Suppose next that $c(u) = c(v) = 2$. Then we may without loss of generality assume that $c(e_{vu}) = 3$ which in turn implies that $c(e_{vw}) = 1$. But then $c(e_{wv}) \ge 4$. Finally, let $c(u) = c(v) = 3$. Assuming without loss of generality that $c(e_{vu}) = 1$, we get $c(e_{vw}) = 2$, $c(e_{wv}) = 1$, but then $c(w) \ge 4$. This settles the case $i=2$.
   764	
   765	Suppose now that $i\ge 3$. Then by Corollary~\ref{cor:subdivisiongraph}, $\pch(S_i(T))\le 4$. We first deal with $i$ odd in which case we need to prove that $\pch(S_i(T))\le 3$ for any tree $T$. In the first subcase assume that $i\equiv 3\pmod 4$.  By Corollary~\ref{cor:subdivisiongraph} we know
   766	that $3 \le \pch(S_i(T))$.  Since $\pch(S_i(T))\le \pch(S_i(K_n))$, where $T$ has order $n$, and $\pch(S_i(K_n))=3$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:SubofKn},
   767	we conclude that $\pch(S_i(T))=3$ when $i\equiv 3\pmod 4$.
   768	The second subcase to consider is when $i\equiv 1\pmod 4$, $i\ge 5$. Again root $S_i(T)$ in a vertex of $T$, say $u$, and consider the corresponding BFS tree. Consider the following sequence $S$ of $i+1$ colors: first repeat the block $2,1,3,1$ as many times as necessary and finish it with colors $2,1,3$. Note that $|S| \equiv 3 \pmod 4$. Let now $e=xy$ be an edge of $T$, where $d_T(x,u) < d_T(y,u)$. If $d_T(x,u)$ is even, then color the vertices in $S_i(T)$ between $x$ and $y$ (including $x$ and $y$) with the sequence of colors $S$, otherwise (if $d_T(x,u)$ is odd), color the vertices in $S_i(T)$ between $x$ and $y$ (including $x$ and $y$) with the sequence of colors obtained by reversing $S$. Note that this gives a well-defined coloring of $V(S_i(T))$, that is, each vertex of $T$ receives a unique color and that $c$ is a packing coloring.
   769	
   770	It remains to consider the case when $i\ge 4$ is even. To complete the argument we need to show that $\pch(S_i(T))\ge 4$ for some tree $T$. Suppose on the contrary that $\pch(S_i(T)) =  3$ holds for any tree $T$ on at least three vertices. If $u$ is a vertex of degree at least $3$, then as above we infer that if $c$ is a $3$-packing coloring of $S_i(T)$, then $c(u) > 1$. In the rest we will also use the fact that if $c(x_1) = 3$ for some vertex of $S_i(T)$, and $x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4, \ldots$  is a path in $S_i(T)$, then $c(x_2) = 1$. Indeed, for otherwise $c(x_2) = 2$, but then $c(x_3) = 1$ and we would have $c(x_4) \ge 4$. Consider an arbitrary edge $uv$ of $T$ and consider the following subcases.
   771	
   772	Let $c(u) = c(v) = 3$. Then the subdivided vertices between $u$ and $v$ must receive the sequence of colors $1,2,1,3,1,2,1,\ldots$. But then the number of subdivided vertices between $u$ and $v$ is odd, a contradiction.
   773	
   774	Let $c(u) = c(v) = 2$ and let $w$ be the vertex adjacent to $u$ on the $u,v$-path. Assume first that $c(w) = 1$. Then the vertices between $u$ and $v$ receive colors $1,3,1,2,1,3,1,\ldots$ which again mean that there are an odd number of these subdivided vertices.  Assume next that $c(w) = 3$. We may assume that $y\ne v$ is another neighbor of $u$ in $T$. Then the neighbor of $u$ on the $u,y$-path in $S_i(T)$ receives color $1$. But then we need color at least $4$ for the next vertex on the $u,y$-path.
